CloudWatch vs Prometheus + Grafana — Battle of Monitoring Giants!
☁️ AWS-native vs 📊 Open-source Excellence

In the modern DevOps and SRE world, selecting the right observability stack is crucial for performance, reliability, and quick incident response.

I recently explored and compared two powerful stacks:


---
 🛠️ CloudWatch (AWS)

Best for: AWS-native workloads

✅ Fully managed

✅ Push-based metric collection

✅ Built-in dashboards, alarms & logs

✅ Integrated with EC2, Lambda, RDS, etc.

✅ Alerting via SNS, email, Lambda

🔒 IAM-based security

💰 Pay-as-you-go model



---

🧠 Prometheus + Grafana (Open Source)

Best for: Kubernetes, microservices, hybrid cloud

✅ Pull-based metric scraping

✅ Flexible and powerful PromQL queries

✅ Advanced visualizations via Grafana

✅ Scalable with Thanos, Cortex

✅ Alerting via Alert manager (Slack, Email, Webhooks)

🌐 Vendor-neutral, community-driven

💸 Free to use (infra costs apply)



---

📊 Tool Comparison Table

Feature	☁️ CloudWatch	📊 Prometheus + Grafana

Type	Managed AWS service	Self-hosted / Open-source
Metric Collection	Push-based	Pull-based
Visualization	CloudWatch Dashboards	Grafana
Alerting System	Alarms + SNS	Alertmanager
Ecosystem Fit	AWS only	Cloud-agnostic/Kubernetes
Custom Metrics	Supported via API	Native
Scalability	Auto-managed	External tools (e.g. Thanos)
Cost	Based on AWS usage	Infra only (Grafana OSS free)



---

🚀 My Verdict:

Choose CloudWatch for seamless AWS monitoring with zero setup.

Choose Prometheus + Grafana for deep observability, customization, and multi-cloud or containerized systems.



---

Monitoring = Visibility = Faster Resolution = Better Uptime
